 { Vladimir Akopian – Alexander Morozevich, Bursa 2010 Learning objective: This game illustrates the typical fight between White’s knight on d5 and Black’s dark-squared bishop. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be3 e5 7. Nf3 Be7 8. Bc4 O-O 9. O-O Qc7 10. Bb3 Be6 11. Qe2 Rc8 12. Rfd1 Nbd7 { We reach a typical position of the structure under discussion. Black can dispute the control of the d5-square and chances are approximately balanced. } 13. Bg5 ( { The alternative } 13. Nh4 $5 { attempting to improve the knight via Nh4-f5-e3-d5, could be met by } g6 $5 { . } ) 
 ( { Or } 13. Rac1 Bc4 14. Bxc4 Qxc4 15. Qxc4 Rxc4 16. Nd2 Rc7 17. f3 { where White’s position seems somewhat preferable, as in the game Schaerer – Rojas Alarcon, Guimaraes 2012. } ) 
b5 14. Ne1 Qb7 15. Bxf6 Nxf6 16. Nd5 Bxd5 17. Bxd5 Nxd5 18. Rxd5 { An interesting position. White has control of the d5-square and Black’s bishop is restricted by its own pawns. However, White’s knight is currently inactive on e1, and Black has good chances for counterplay based on the c-file. Overall, the position is approximately equal, though precise play is needed from Black in order to avoid falling into a passive position after a typical c2-c3, Nc2-e3-d5 with a big advantage. } ( { But not } 18. exd5 $2 f5 $15 { when Black has a superior version of the previous chapter’s structure, since White cannot expand on the queenside. } ) 
f5 $5 { Hoping to create counterplay along the f-file. } ( { A good alternative was } 18... Rc4 19. Rad1 ( { but not } 18... f5 18. c3 $6 f5 $1 ) 
 ( { PH: The computer indicates that White should play } 18... f5 18. b3 { , not a happy move to make, but after } Rc3 20. a4 b4 21. Rad1 Rac8 22. R1d3 $5 { White can perhaps exchange a pair of rooks and ease the pressure against c2. } ) 
Rac8 { when it is unclear how White can bring his knight to d5: } 20. c3 ( { or } 19... f5 19. a3 a5 ) 
 { Hoping for Nc2-e3/b4-d5, but after } b4 $1 21. cxb4 Qxb4 22. f3 h5 $5 { it remains unclear how White is to get his knight to d5 . } ) 
 ( { PH: Given the note about 18...Rc4 } 18... f5 18. b3 { , perhaps better was } Rc5 19. Rad1 ( { The immediate exchange is nothing to worry about: } 18... f5 18. Rxc5 dxc5 { would allow ...c4 followed by ...Bc5 next. } ) 
