b4 { 21.b3 White is solid, but compared to the 18...Rc4 19.b3 line, the a2-pawn is exposed here as well. For example } g6 22. h4 Kg7 { 23.Rxc5 Rxc5 intending ...Qc6 and ...Ra5 with pressure on White's a2 and c2-pawns. } ) 
19. Rad1 fxe4 20. Qxe4 Rc4 21. Qe2 Rac8 $2 ( { Better was } 21... Rf8 22. c3 Rcf4 23. f3 g5 $5 { with mutual chances, for example } 24. h3 h5 { (threatening ...g5-g4) } 25. Rxd6 $5 Bxd6 26. Rxd6 Qg7 27. Nd3 R4f6 28. Qxe5 g4 $5 { with complications. } ) 
 ( { Or even } 21... Rf4 22. f3 Raf8 23. g3 R4f7 24. Qe4 Qc8 25. Kg2 h5 { and Black has active counterplay. } ) 
22. a3 ( { PH: in order to prevent Black's ...Rf4 idea, it was worth playing } 22. g3 $1 a5 23. h4 { , first improving White's control over the position. } ) 
