[Event "Chess Structures – A Grandmaster Guide"]
[Site "https://www.chessable.com/variation/1551071/"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "10.12"]
[White "9) Najdorf Type II"]
[Black "Sergei Tiviakov vs. Viswanathan Anand"]
[Result "1-0"]

 { Sergei Tiviakov – Viswanathan Anand, Germany 2012 Learning objectives: 1. This game is an example of how White can gain a steady control of the d5-square after c2-c4 is played. 2. White goes on to dominate the board, and this game shows how to convert such an advantage into a full point. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. Bb5+ Nd7 4. d4 cxd4 5. Qxd4 a6 6. Bxd7+ Bxd7 7. c4 e5 8. Qd3 { We have reached the thematic position. In contrast to the previous games, the pawns on c4 and e4 automatically secure control of the d5-square for White. Hence Black should find very concrete counterplay in order to avoid a passive position. } h6 $6 { This is a logical move; Black wants to develop his knight without having to worry about the positional threat of Bg5xf6, but this is just too slow and this move has disappeared from top play. } ( { Nowadays } 8... b5 $1 { is the preferred move; it was employed for example in the game Carlsen – Anand, Sandes 2013. In the next game we will study a very similar position. } ) 
 ( { If } 8... Nf6 $6 9. Bg5 $1 { followed by Nc3 and Bxf6 , gaining a long-term advantage due to the control of the d5-square. } ) 
9. Nc3 Nf6 10. O-O Be7 ( { As Tiviakov points out, } 10... b5 $6 11. cxb5 axb5 12. Nxb5 Nxe4 $2 { does not work because of: } 13. Qxe4 Bxb5 14. Nxe5 $1 { +- } ) 
11. a4 { Further preventing the key ...b7-b5 break. White should not worry about the weakening of the b4-square, as Black cannot make use of it. } b6 $6 { As GM Tiviakov pointed out in his analysis, this move is a key mistake for two reasons. First, the b6-pawn will become a weakness, and second, this imprecision gives White just enough time to play b2-b3 followed by Ba3 with pressure against d6. } ( { Better was: } 11... O-O 12. Be3 ( { now there is no time for } 11... b6 11. b3 $6 { due to } b5 $1 13. axb5 ( { or } 12... b6 12. cxb5 axb5 14. Nxb5 Bxb5 15. Qxb5 Nxe4 ) 
axb5 14. Rxa8 Qxa8 15. cxb5 Rb8 { with equal chances in Dobrowolski – Miton, Lubniewice 2005 } { _ } ) 
Qc7 13. a5 Rac8 14. b3 Be6 { Black had a playable position in Mastrovasilis – Indjic, Kragujevac 2013. } ) 
12. b3 { After this move Black is permanently restricted, since the ...b6-b5 break is well prevented, the d6-pawn is weak, and the d5-square is under White’s control. } Ra7 { Intending to protect the d6-pawn after a future ...Rd7. } ( { Another option was } 12... Bc6 13. Ba3 Ra7 14. Rfd1 Rd7 15. Nd2 { followed by the standard Nf1-e3-d5 with positional dominance, as in the game Petr – Duda, Czech Championship 2012. } ) 
 ( { More normal was } 12... Qc7 13. Ba3 Rd8 14. Rfd1 Bc8 { and now } 15. Bb4 $5 { ⩲ preparing a4-a5, with promising chances for White. } ) 
13. Rd1 { Threatening to win a pawn with Ba3 followed by Bxd6. } Bc8 { Too passive, as the bishop is not of much use on c8. } ( { The alternative } 13... Bg4 { seems reasonable, trying to eliminate the knight before it becomes active with the typical Nd2-f1-e3-d5, however after } 14. Ba3 Rd7 15. Rd2 { White has a big advantage anyway. A possible threat is Ne1-c2-e3-d5. } ) 
14. Ba3 Rd7 15. Nd2 { Taking the route Nd2-f1-e3-d5. } ( { An alternative route was } 15. Ne1 $5 { followed by Nc2-e3-d5. } ) 
O-O 16. Nf1 Bb7 ( { It makes sense to try } 16... Nh5 $5 { hoping for counterplay after ...Nf4-e6 . However, after the precise } 17. g3 $1 { covering the f4-square, the knight is useless on h5 , while White has not created any weaknesses that Black can take advantage of. } Bb7 18. Ne3 Nf6 19. Nf5 $16 { with a dominating position. } ) 
17. Ne3 Re8 ( { Here again } 17... Nh5 18. Ned5 Bg5 $16 { was called for } ( 18... Nf4 19. Qe3 Nxd5 20. Nxd5 Bxd5 21. Rxd5 f5 22. Rad1 fxe4 23. Qxe4 Rf4 24. Qe2 $16 ) 
 ) 
18. Ncd5 Nxd5 19. Nxd5 Bxd5 ( { More principled was } 19... Bg5 { hoping to do something useful with the dark-squared bishop, but after } 20. g3 $5 { followed by h2-h4, the bishop will probably have to return to e7 . } ) 
20. Qxd5 $16 { White has achieved a typical dream position for this structure. All of Black’s pieces are very passive and the d6-pawn is permanently weak. } ( { Of course not } 20. cxd5 $2 { losing the strong d5-square and giving safety to the d6-pawn. Black would reply } Qa8 $1 { (Tiviakov) (followed by placing rooks on the c-file with excellent chances to hold a draw.) } ) 
Qa8 $6 { Not a good practical decision, as now White can expand his kingside without any worries. } ( { The move } 20... Bg5 $2 { hoping to play ...Re6 protecting the d6-pawn, is met by } 21. a5 $1 ( { or } 20... Qa8 20. Qc6 $5 { as suggested by Tiviakov, with the idea of Rd5 followed by Rad1 } ) 
bxa5 22. c5 Qa8 23. cxd6 { when the passed pawn on d6 should decide the game. } ) 
 ( { Preferable is } 20... Qc8 $5 { keeping queens on the board, in case a chance for counterplay arises. } ) 
21. Qxa8 Rxa8 22. Rd5 f6 ( { Similar is } 22... Rc8 23. Rad1 Rc6 24. g3 { intending to follow up with f2-f4-f5, but not: } 24. Rxe5 $2 dxe5 25. Rxd7 Bxa3 $19 ) 
23. Rad1 Rad8 24. g3 $1 { The plan is f2-f4-f5, restraining even more the action of Black’s dark-squared bishop. } g5 ( { If } 24... Kf7 25. f4 g6 26. f5 gxf5 27. exf5 { White has a huge advantage, and may proceed with Kf2-f3, Bc1-e3, g3-g4 and h2-h4. Meanwhile, there is little for Black to do. } ) 
25. f4 $1 { Provoking the creation of more weaknesses. } gxf4 26. gxf4 Kf7 27. Kf2 { A prophylactic move against ...Rg8-g4. } ( { Premature is } 27. f5 $2 Rg8 28. Kf2 Rg4 { when White has lost most of his advantage, for example: } 29. Re1 Rf4 30. Kg2 Rd8 $5 ) 
exf4 { A very sad decision, accepting the creation of new weaknesses on f6 and h6. } ( { Waiting with } 27... h5 { also does not help. For example, } 28. Kf3 Ke6 29. f5 Kf7 30. Rg1 Rg8 31. Rxg8 Kxg8 32. Kg3 $1 { followed by Kh4 , threatening the h5-pawn. } ) 
28. Kf3 Ke6 29. Bc1 Rc8 30. Bxf4 Bf8 31. h4 { Followed by h4-h5, fixing the h6-pawn on the colour of the attacking bishop. } Rc6 32. h5 $18 { The game is won for White, and the rest is an exhibition of Tiviakov’s endgame technique. } Rb7 33. Be3 { Rather than searching for direct ways to realize the advantage, White should slowly improve each one of his pieces. In this case, the bishop on d4 is strong, pressing against the b6- and f6-pawns. } Rb8 34. Bd4 Rc7 35. Rg1 Bg7 ( { Or } 35... Rg7 36. Rg6 $1 Rf7 ( { but not } 36... Rxg6 $6 37. hxg6 $18 { with a strong passed pawn; even White’s king is a threat after Kg4-h5 } ) 
37. Rf5 $18 { is similar to the game. } ) 
36. Rg6 Rf7 37. Be3 Rd7 38. Rf5 ( { Much better was } 38. e5 $1 { winning directly: } Kf7 ( { but not } 38. Rf5 dxe5 $2 39. Rxd7 Kxd7 40. Rxg7 $18 ) 
39. exf6 $18 { since } Bxf6 $2 { loses to } 40. Rf5 ) 
Rf7 39. Kg4 ( { More logical was } 39. Ke2 $5 { This will be followed by Kd3-c3, b3-b4-b5, etc. White’s queenside pawns have much more winning potential than the h5-pawn, thus it makes sense to play on this flank. } ) 
Bf8 40. Bxh6 Bxh6 41. Rxh6 Rg7+ ( { A much better defence was } 41... Rg8 $1 42. Rg6 Rxg6 43. hxg6 Rf8 { though White should still be winning after } 44. a5 $1 ) 
42. Kf4 Rf8 43. a5 { White is winning and I leave the rest of the game without annotations, as it bears no relevance to the topic of this chapter. } Rg1 44. axb6 Rb1 45. b7 Rxb3 46. e5 dxe5+ 47. Rxe5+ Kd6 48. Rf5 Ke6 49. Rh7 Rd8 50. Rd5 { Final remarks 1. Black accepted an inferior position when he missed his chance to play 8...b5 to challenge White’s centre. Later he worsened his position with the weakening (or at least useless) 11...b6. 2. Around move 16 Black should have played ...Nh5 in order to attempt ...f7-f5 or ...Bg5 to create reasonable counterplay. Instead, trading knights on d5 eliminated his chances to create any serious threats later in the game. 3. The moves 19... Bxd5 and 20... Qa8 only crystallized White’s advantage by reducing the fight down to just the protagonists of the positional imbalance. Black’s weak d6-pawn, and the qualitative difference between White’s active bishop on a3 and Black’s passive bishop on e7, reduced Black’s chances to a minimum. White won the endgame convincingly, as there was little Black could do to hold it. } 
 1-0

