Bc4 30. Bxc4 Qxc4 { An important moment in the game. Over the last several moves White has disregarded many good tactical opportunities in order to reach a superior endgame. Is this approach correct? In my opinion it is not. The resulting endgame, although superior for White, offers Black good chances to survive, as we will analyze. } 31. Rd5 $1 { The best option. } ( { Instead } 31. Qxc4 $2 Rxc4 32. Rd5 Kd7 { leaves White in a position that, although preferable, contains few winning chances since a queenside expansion is impossible. } ) 
Kd7 { This move gives White an extra opportunity to keep queens on the board. } ( { The correct reaction was: } 31... Qxb3 $1 32. axb3 Rc6 33. Red1 { Threatening Be1xb4 . } h5 $1 ( { Another option was } 33... O-O 34. Be1 Rb8 { with reasonable chances to hold the game. } ) 
 ( { Black would like to react with } 33... Kd7 34. Be1 Rb8 ) 
 ( { but } 33... Kd7 { is refuted by } 34. Rxe5 { . } ) 
34. g5 f6 35. g6 ( { Or } 34... Kd7 34. gxf6 gxf6 36. Rg1 Kf7 37. Be1 Rb8 { with good chances to hold the endgame. } ) 
Kd7 { This is the point! The e5-pawn is now immune. } 36. Ra5 Rhc8 37. Rd2 Bd8 { Black should have no problems drawing this endgame. } ) 
32. Ra5 ( { An interesting computer-like move was } 32. Qa4 Rc6 33. Bc5 $1 { when the complications seem to favour White, though it would be difficult to evaluate in a real game. } ) 
Qxb3 33. axb3 ( { Or } 33. cxb3 Rc6 34. Rd1 Rhc8 { which gives Black good defensive resources as } 35. Rxe5 $2 { is well met by } Rc2 36. Bb6 Bf6 { with the initiative. } ) 
